<onlyinclude>'''Information ecology''' is a way of studying how people, technologies, institutions and everyday practices shape the creation, circulation and use of [[Information/Simple|information]]. The ecological comparison emphasizes interactions and surroundings rather than treating information management as a problem of computer storage alone.

== An everyday example ==
In a hospital, a laboratory result can pass through an instrument, a database, a nurse and a physician. The result is useful only if it is accurate, reaches the appropriate person, arrives in time and can be understood in context. The software, working routines, responsibilities and trust among people all matter. Changing one part can change the rest of the system.

== Two influential approaches ==
Thomas Davenport and Laurence Prusak used information ecology to describe information management across whole organizations, including human behavior and organizational politics. Bonnie Nardi and Vicki O'Day used the idea of local information ecologies to examine technologies as parts of human practices and values. Both approaches reject the assumption that installing more technology by itself solves information problems.

== Why it matters ==
Information ecology can help identify why people cannot find what they need, why records are duplicated, why information is withheld or why useful tools are abandoned. It can guide changes to workflows, documentation and access. The ecological analogy is not a literal claim that records are organisms or that organizational information follows the laws of biological ecosystems.

